thread:
joanne: Observing spikes in Rust client memory on prod when many SSE streams send fragmented JSON. Buffer keeps growing until a full object arrives, then allocator jumps. Anyone seen this pattern?

kevin: Can you paste the read/parse loop? my money is on BytesMut growth + naive serde_json::from_slice usage.

joanne: Here’s the simplified chunk handler we use:
```rust
let mut buf = BytesMut::new();
loop {
    let n = socket.read_buf(&mut buf).await?;
    if n == 0 { break; }
    match serde_json::from_slice::<Value>(&buf) {
        Ok(v) => { handle_json(v); buf.clear(); }
        Err(_) => { /* keep accumulating */ }
    }
}
```

ashok: yup that explains it. serde_json::from_slice will error on partial data and you keep appending, so buf can grow unbounded under adversarial chunking. Use an incremental parser or stream deserializer.

ben: Two quick mitigation options: 1) hard cap buffer size and drop/reset when exceeded, 2) use incremental parsing (StreamDeserializer) so you consume only complete items. For SSE you can also try scanning for a top-level newline boundary if the protocol allows.

sara: For tokio specifically you can pause reads by not polling the socket until the parser consumes bytes — basically apply backpressure at the read task. If reader is on its own task, yield or await a notify from the parser.

joanne: Good ideas. Any sample code for StreamDeserializer approach?

ashok: Example:
```rust
use serde_json::de::StreamDeserializer;
let mut de = StreamDeserializer::new(serde_json::de::IoRead::new(&mut reader));
for value in de {
    match value {
        Ok(v) => handle_json(v),
        Err(e) => { log::debug!("partial or bad json: {}", e); break; }
    }
}
```
This avoids copying into one giant buffer, but you may need to wrap the SSE framing into an adapter that yields raw JSON objects to the deserializer.

kevin: Another pragmatic guard:
```rust
const MAX_BUF: usize = 256 * 1024; // tunable
if buf.len() > MAX_BUF {
    metrics::increment("sse.buffer_overflow");
    // either reset connection or return a specific error to caller
    return Err(Error::BufferExceeded);
}
```
That buys time while we roll a better parser.

joanne: I can add that guard quickly. Want to include a metric for buffer_bytes and buffer_overflow. Also we should tag streams that hit the guard so product can see impact. :eyes: 

ben: +1. For long-term let's add a parser task that pushes parsed items to a bounded mpsc channel; the reader fills a small buffer and yields when channel full — natural backpressure.

sara: Keep an eye on task scheduling — spawn_blocking for heavy parsing if it blocks. Also validate with a synthetic load: randomize chunk sizes, emit partial braces to repro.

joanne: I’ll open a tiny PR with the max-buffer guard, metrics, and a short test that fuzzes chunk boundaries. Assigning to kevin for review.

